WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:17.563
Priya Raman: Okay, thanks for jumping on so fast. Sandra, Mark, Paul is on with us from support. Sandra, you wanted to walk us through what you found.

2
00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:32.115
Sandra Pruitt: Yes. I was doing a routine audit Friday afternoon, pulling transcripts from the central scheduling queue, and I found a call where the patient's information was sitting right there in the transcript, plain as day.

3
00:00:33.481 --> 00:00:35.832
Priya Raman: Okay. Can you read me what's exposed?

4
00:00:36.726 --> 00:00:44.922
Sandra Pruitt: I'll read it exactly as it appears. Patient name Margaret Ellison, date of birth March 14, 1958, MRN 4471229083.

5
00:00:45.626 --> 00:00:49.451
Mark Delgado: That's a scheduler call. She called in to move an appointment.

6
00:00:49.897 --> 00:00:57.927
Sandra Pruitt: There's more. The agent read back the callback number (615) 555-0148, and her home address, 2218 Belmont Boulevard, Nashville.

7
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.376
Priya Raman: Okay. I'm writing this down as you go.

8
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:11.609
Sandra Pruitt: Spoken dates of birth and MRNs are coming through in the transcripts completely unredacted. Card numbers get masked, but those don't.

9
00:01:12.365 --> 00:01:16.940
Mark Delgado: Sandra, how many calls are we talking about, just this one?

10
00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:28.139
Sandra Pruitt: I've spot-checked about forty so far and found four with the same pattern. I stopped there because I wanted to get on this call before I went further.

11
00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:33.629
Paul Ricci: That's helpful. Can I ask a couple of questions about the configuration while we're here?

12
00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:35.559
Sandra Pruitt: Please.

13
00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:42.273
Paul Ricci: Which redaction settings are currently turned on for the account? Is it the standard profile or something custom?

14
00:01:42.722 --> 00:01:54.949
Mark Delgado: As far as I know it's whatever we set up at implementation. I haven't touched it. I can pull it up if you tell me where to look.

15
00:01:55.234 --> 00:02:03.189
Paul Ricci: I'll send you the exact path after this call. And just to confirm on my side, card numbers are being masked correctly?

16
00:02:03.847 --> 00:02:09.155
Mark Delgado: Yes. We tested that ourselves, the payment calls show the card as masked. That part's fine.

17
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:15.976
Paul Ricci: Good. So the masking is working for card data but not for the spoken identifiers.

18
00:02:16.211 --> 00:02:28.711
Sandra Pruitt: That's the shape of it. And I'll say this now while everyone's here. We need to be able to choose which identifiers get redacted, and lock that setting so a regional admin can't turn it off.

19
00:02:28.976 --> 00:02:39.481
Priya Raman: Understood. I'm not going to guess at what's possible on that, I want to take it to the product team and come back with something concrete.

20
00:02:40.713 --> 00:02:48.176
Sandra Pruitt: I appreciate that, but I need more than concrete eventually. We have a BAA. This is reportable if it goes wider.

21
00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:58.592
Priya Raman: I know. And I'm sorry this happened. Let's figure out what we can do right now to stop the bleeding, then work the rest.

22
00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:03.021
Mark Delgado: Can we turn transcripts off for the scheduling queue in the meantime?

23
00:03:03.626 --> 00:03:14.765
Paul Ricci: That's a config change, so I don't want to promise it on this call. Let me look at what's available for that queue specifically and get back to you today.

24
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:21.558
Mark Delgado: Today would be good. I've got agents on that queue around the clock.

25
00:03:22.816 --> 00:03:35.836
Paul Ricci: Understood. I'll also want to know whether the calls in question came in through the main tracking number or a campaign number, so I can scope how far back this goes.

26
00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:42.825
Mark Delgado: Most of our scheduling calls come through the main line. The campaign numbers are more for the clinics' outreach.

27
00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:51.056
Sandra Pruitt: I can send you the four call IDs I found. Should I send them to you directly, Paul?

28
00:03:51.755 --> 00:03:57.818
Paul Ricci: Yes, that works. Send them to me and I'll attach them to the ticket.

29
00:03:58.056 --> 00:04:02.411
Priya Raman: Sandra, who else at Meridian knows about this so far?

30
00:04:02.947 --> 00:04:07.665
Sandra Pruitt: Just Mark and our general counsel. I haven't told the clinic leads yet.

31
00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:15.167
Priya Raman: Okay. I'll keep this tight on our side. Paul, me, and my director, that's it for now.

32
00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.434
Sandra Pruitt: Good. I don't want this bouncing around.

33
00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:29.724
Priya Raman: Here's what I can commit to. By end of day tomorrow you'll have a written summary from me of what we know and what we're doing.

34
00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:37.861
Priya Raman: By Thursday I'll have an answer from product on the redaction controls. And Paul will have the transcript question answered today.

35
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.319
Paul Ricci: I'll confirm that in the ticket before I sign off today.

36
00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.361
Sandra Pruitt: And the exposed records? Do we need to do anything with those transcripts?

37
00:04:50.358 --> 00:05:01.297
Priya Raman: I'll ask about options for those specific calls in the same conversation. I don't want to tell you something today that I can't stand behind.

38
00:05:02.782 --> 00:05:06.903
Mark Delgado: That's fair. I'd rather you come back straight with us than guess.

39
00:05:08.355 --> 00:05:18.469
Sandra Pruitt: Agreed. One more thing, and this is procedural. Any communication about this needs to come to me and to Mark, not to the clinic managers.

40
00:05:19.792 --> 00:05:24.441
Priya Raman: Noted. Paul, anything else you need from them before we wrap?

41
00:05:25.095 --> 00:05:30.719
Paul Ricci: Just the call IDs and confirmation of the queue name. I have the rest.

42
00:05:31.625 --> 00:05:34.576
Mark Delgado: I'll send the queue name right after this.

43
00:05:36.022 --> 00:05:45.267
Priya Raman: Okay. Sandra, thank you for bringing it to us quickly. I know this isn't how you wanted to spend a Monday.

44
00:05:46.441 --> 00:05:51.339
Sandra Pruitt: No, it isn't. But here we are. I'll watch for your summary tomorrow.

45
00:05:52.644 --> 00:05:54.464
Priya Raman: You'll have it. Talk soon.
